          <h2>The benefit categories behind WellFinder treatments</h2>
          <p>Canada Life group plans pay for most wellness care through paramedical coverage. Each visit draws from the category that matches the practitioner who treated you, so a session with a chiropractor and a session with a physiotherapist come out of different balances. The categories that matter most for WellFinder readers are:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Physiotherapy, which covers pelvic floor physiotherapy, vestibular rehabilitation, shockwave therapy and bike fitting assessments</li>
            <li>Chiropractic, which covers many clinical Pilates and golf swing assessment clinics that bill through a chiropractor</li>
            <li>Massage therapy, which covers TMJ massage, lymphatic drainage and head spa treatments given by a registered massage therapist</li>
            <li>Naturopathy, which covers microneedling, IV and IM vitamin therapy and fertility consultations with a naturopathic doctor</li>
            <li>Acupuncture, which covers fertility acupuncture, cosmetic acupuncture and facial gua sha from a registered acupuncturist</li>
            <li>Psychology, which covers couples therapy, sleep coaching, mental performance coaching and psychoeducational assessments</li>
            <li>Speech therapy, which covers singing voice therapy, accent modification and stuttering therapy</li>
            <li>Dietitian services, which cover sports nutrition consultations with a registered dietitian</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Your plan booklet lists which categories you have and the maximum for each. Many plans pay a percentage of each visit up to an annual maximum, and many apply reasonable and customary limits, which are the amounts Canada Life considers typical for a service in your area. A charge above that limit can leave part of the bill with you.</p>

          <h2>How claiming works with Canada Life</h2>
          <p>Members manage claims through My Canada Life at Work, on the web at mycanadalifeatwork.ca or in the mobile app of the same name. Long time members may remember the portal by its earlier name, GroupNet for Plan Members. You sign in, start a claim, choose who the claim is for, and attach a photo or scan of your receipt. The account also holds your benefits card, your banking details for direct deposit and your coverage and balance information. A steady routine helps:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Add your banking information once, so claim payments arrive by direct deposit</li>
            <li>Keep the receipt from every visit, showing the practitioner, their credentials, the date and the amount paid</li>
            <li>Submit the claim soon after the visit and watch its status in the app</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Canada Life also supports direct submission through some providers. Pharmacies and dental offices do this routinely, and some paramedical providers can submit your claim to Canada Life at the appointment, so you pay only the part your plan leaves with you. Ask the clinic before your visit whether it offers this.</p>

          <h2>The health care spending account</h2>
          <p>Many Canada Life plans include a Health Care Spending Account, usually shortened to HCSA. The account is a pool of credits from your employer for eligible health and dental expenses, following the list the Canada Revenue Agency allows.</p>
          <p>The order of claiming matters. Canada Life treats the HCSA as the last payer, so you claim under your main plan first, and under a spouse plan if you have one, before the account pays what remains. In My Canada Life at Work you can often send one submission that runs the expense through your main plan and then applies the leftover amount to the HCSA. Spending account deadlines are often earlier than main plan deadlines, so check your balance and dates in the app.</p>

          <h2>WellFinder treatments and how they may bill under a Canada Life plan</h2>
          <p>Costs below are typical Canadian prices collected for the WellFinder directory. Your plan pays according to its own maximums and limits, so confirm the amounts for each category in your account.</p>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Treatment</th><th>Usually billed under</th><th>Typical cost in Canada</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Pelvic Floor Physiotherapy</td><td>Physiotherapy</td><td>$110 to $185 per session</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Golf Swing Assessment</td><td>Physiotherapy or Chiropractic</td><td>$100 to $250 per assessment</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Clinical Pilates</td><td>Physiotherapy or Chiropractic</td><td>$90 to $160 per session</td></tr>
              <tr><td>TMJ Massage</td><td>Massage Therapy</td><td>$80 to $130 per session</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Microneedling</td><td>Naturopathy</td><td>$200 to $350 per session</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Fertility Acupuncture</td><td>Acupuncture</td><td>$90 to $140 per session</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Psychoeducational Assessments</td><td>Psychology</td><td>Varies by provider</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Sports Nutrition</td><td>Dietitian</td><td>$100 to $175 per consultation</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>WellFinder does not publish a typical price for psychoeducational assessments, since fees depend on the scope of the assessment. Ask the clinic for its fee before you commit.</p>

          <h2>Questions members ask</h2>
          <h3>Does Canada Life cover chiropractic care?</h3>
          <p>Many Canada Life group plans include chiropractic care as a paramedical category, usually with an annual maximum and a limit on the amount paid per visit. Your account shows your own maximums and your remaining balance, which is the reliable place to check before a series of visits.</p>
          <h3>Why did my plan pay less than the full receipt?</h3>
          <p>Common reasons are a percentage paid per visit, an annual maximum that is nearly used up, and reasonable and customary limits that cap what the plan counts as the cost of a visit in your area. Your claim statement in My Canada Life at Work explains how each amount was reached.</p>
          <h3>Can my massage clinic bill Canada Life directly?</h3>
          <p>Some can. Canada Life accepts claims submitted directly by some paramedical providers, and clinics that offer this will ask for your policy and certificate numbers at the visit. If your clinic does not submit for you, pay at the visit and claim in the app with your receipt.</p>
          <h3>When should I use my Health Care Spending Account?</h3>
          <p>Use it after your main plan, and a spouse plan if one applies, have paid their parts. Watch the account deadline in the app, since spending account claims often close earlier than main plan claims.</p>

          <h2>Find care that fits your Canada Life plan</h2>
          <p>Begin with the treatment, then confirm the category in your account. Browse the <a href="/#directory">WellFinder directory</a> to compare clinics across Canada, and open pages such as <a href="/golf">golf swing assessment clinics</a> and <a href="/clinics-vestibular-rehabilitation">vestibular rehabilitation clinics</a> to see providers and how they bill. Before you book, sign in to My Canada Life at Work to check the balance for that category and ask the clinic what will appear on your receipt.</p>
          <p>WellFinder is an independent directory and is not affiliated with Canada Life. This guide explains common features of Canada Life group plans in Canada. Your employer plan governs your coverage, and Canada Life or your plan administrator can confirm what your plan pays.</p>

